  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.3. Increased institutional capacity of labour administrations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In June 2021, the Ministry of Labour and Social Security (MLSS) through the labour inspectorate department adopted and started implementing the Strategic Compliance Model (SCM) in the security sector. The MLSS has since developed a Strategic Compliance Plan on the Strategic Compliance Portal that has a set of interventions including enforcement, education, systemic and  political and is assisting the labour inspectorate in improving compliance. The plan respects the core elements of labour inspection as defined by Convention No. 81 and No. 129. To implement the plan, the strategic compliance task team has engaged various stakeholders including the Tripartite Consultative Labour Council, workers&apos; and employers&apos; organizations, community radio stations and the Zambia Occupational Safety and Health institute, and the MLSS Productivity and Planning Department. The systemic intervention (revision of checklist 18C) has been conducted collaboratively across departments in the MLSS and regions.
51% (15/29) of Strategic Compliance actions devised in the plan for Zambia have been completed. This includes 100% of the systemic intervention, 83% of the education intervention, and 33% of the political intervention. The enforcement campaign is anticipated to be completed as initial steps have been taken.
In addition, the plan has contributed towards having an increased number of duty holders adopting compliant workplace practices through follow up inspection visits that determine that there is a correction of a noncompliance detected in the previous visit. In December 2020, the ILO convened a training for Labour Inspectors on Strategic Compliance as a way of introducing the Strategic Compliance Planning methodology to achieve compliance outcomes in light of mismatched powers and a need to shoulder greater responsibility for enforcing compliance in the ever-evolving world of work, provided guidance to the labour inspectorate to develop a Strategic Compliance Plan based on available resources and pilot it in selected priority sectors focusing on selected priority issues and demonstrated how to use the Strategic Compliance (SC) portal. The SC Portal enables participating labour inspectorates to upload and analyze the results of innovative compliance and enforcement strategies deployed in priority sectors. An interactive dashboard enables data visualization and evidence-based decision making for the design of future interventions.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
